{"question": "A 35-year-old Caucasian female presents to the emergency room with unilateral leg swelling. She reports that she developed painful left-sided leg swelling and redness earlier in the day. She is otherwise healthy and takes no medications. She denies any recent prolonged travel. She experienced a similar episode affecting the opposite leg one year ago and was diagnosed with a thrombus in the right femoral vein. On examination, the left leg is erythematous and swollen. Passive dorsiflexion of the left ankle elicits pain in the left calf. Ultrasound of the leg reveals a thrombus in the left popliteal vein. A genetic workup reveals that she has an inherited condition. What is the most likely pathophysiology of this patient\u2019s condition?", "options": {"A": "Elevated serum homocysteine", "B": "Thrombin inhibitor deficiency", "C": "Deficiency in protein C", "D": "Auto-antibodies directed against phospholipids", "E": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Vascular Medicine", "Thrombosis", "Hematology", "Genetic Medicine", "Emergency Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Hematology", "Vascular Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Vascular Medicine": "Based on the medical scenario provided, the patient presents with a history of unilateral leg swelling, redness, and pain, which are classic symptoms of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). The patient has a history of a similar episode in the past, which was diagnosed as a thrombus in the right femoral vein. The current episode is affecting the left leg, and the ultrasound confirms a thrombus in the left popliteal vein. The patient has a genetic predisposition, which suggests an inherited thrombophilia. ### Pathophysiology\nThe most likely pathophysiology in this case is the development of a thrombus in the left popliteal vein, which is a continuation of the thrombus that occurred in the right femoral vein. The thrombus in the femoral vein likely led to a clot